==================================================
Lab 2: Subverting Application Logic (Login Bypass)
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

An application lets users log in with a username and a password. When someone submits a username and password,
the website checks them against the database using a SQL query. If the query finds a matching user, the login is
allowed. If not, the login is rejected.

Goal: log in as the administrator without knowing the administrator password.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS

If a user submits the username wiener and the password bluecheese, the website runs this query:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ‘wiener’ AND password = ‘bluecheese’

This means:

get all columns
from the users table
only the row where username is wiener
and only if password is bluecheese

If a row is returned, the login is successful. If nothing is returned, the login fails.

=======================
WHAT IS THE PROBLEM

The username and password are placed directly into the query without being checked or cleaned. This means we can
use the same trick as before, using a single quote and two dashes, to change what the query actually checks.

======================================
THE ATTACK: BYPASS THE PASSWORD CHECK

Instead of a real username, we send administrator followed by a single quote and two dashes, and we leave the
password blank.

The query becomes:

SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ‘administrator’--‘ AND password = ‘’

The two dashes comment out everything after them, so the password check is completely removed. The query now
only checks if the username is administrator. Since that user exists, the query returns their details, and we
are logged in as administrator, without ever knowing their real password.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and go to the login page.
In the username field, type: administrator followed by a single quote, then two dashes.
Leave the password field empty, or type anything, it does not matter.
Click the login button. You are now logged in as administrator, and the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the re library, used for searching text patterns, and the requests library, used for sending web requests.

A session is created at the start. A session remembers cookies between requests, the same way a browser keeps you
logged in across pages. This matters here because the login form needs a token tied to that same session.

It asks you to paste the address of the login page, and saves it as login_url.

It sends a request to that login page and saves the page’s HTML content, so the script can read it.

It searches that HTML for a hidden field called csrf, using a pattern match, and pulls out its value. This value
is called a CSRF token.

A CSRF token is a random value that websites generate for each visitor, to make sure form submissions are
genuinely coming from their own pages and not from somewhere else. Without sending back the correct token, the
login form submission would be rejected, so the script must grab this token first before trying to log in.

If the token is found, the script prints it and continues. If it is not found, the script prints an error and
stops completely.

It builds the payload to submit. This includes the CSRF token it just found, the username set to administrator
followed by a single quote and two dashes, and a password. In this script the password is set to admin12345,
though in the actual attack the password value does not matter, since it gets removed from the query by the
two dashes anyway.

It sends this payload to the login page using a POST request, through the same session, so any cookies set
during login are kept.

It then builds the address of the home page by taking the login page address and replacing the word login with
nothing, giving the site’s main address.

It requests that home page using the same session, so it is viewed as the logged in administrator.

Finally, it checks the home page content for the sentence the lab displays when solved. If found, it prints
success. If not, it prints a failure message and suggests the lab link may have expired.

=============================
WHY THE SESSION MATTERS

A normal requests.get call on its own does not remember cookies between separate calls. Using session instead
means that once the login request succeeds and the website gives back a login cookie, that same cookie is
automatically included in the next request to the home page. Without the session, the home page request would
look like a fresh, logged out visitor, and the success check would fail even if the login itself worked.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The website builds the login query by joining the username and password directly into the query text. Since the
input is never separated from the actual SQL instructions, a single quote and two dashes placed inside the
username are enough to change the entire meaning of the query.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries, also called prepared statements, so the username and password are always treated as
plain data, never as part of the SQL instructions, no matter what characters they contain.

Other good practices:

Never reveal through error messages whether a username exists or not.
Apply rate limiting or account lockouts on repeated failed login attempts.
Use proper password hashing and comparison on the server side.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

A single quote and two dashes can remove entire parts of a query, including password checks.
This type of attack does not need to guess a password at all, it just needs to know or guess a valid username.
A session keeps the login cookie between requests, which is necessary to check if the login actually worked.
